UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 37, Lesson 1: Finding and recording sources', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Good sources are chosen by asking who made them, when, why and whether they can be trusted. Each source is recorded with its title, author or creator, date and where it was found. Paraphrasing means putting ideas in your own words and naming the source, instead of copying.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhich source is more reliable for the date a school opened: a school history written by the school, or a comment on a website with no author?\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe school history, because it names who made it and was written by people with records. The unnamed comment cannot be traced.\n\nWATCH FOR\nCopying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFind three sources for your inquiry question. Complete a source record for each with creator, date and where found, and write one paraphrased fact from each. Add one question about how far you trust each source.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Good sources are chosen by asking who made them, when, why and whether they can be trusted. Each source is recorded with its title, author or creator, date and where it was found. Paraphrasing means putting ideas in your own words and naming the source, instead of copying.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Find three sources for your inquiry question. Complete a source record for each with creator, date and where found, and write one paraphrased fact from each. Add one question about how far you trust each source. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: The school history, because it names who made it and was written by people with records. The unnamed comment cannot be traced. Reasoning: I used source by attending to record and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Copying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own. Correction: The school history, because it names who made it and was written by people with records. The unnamed comment cannot be traced. Why: the correction restores accurate use of source.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Copying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating source on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with source, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The school history, because it names who made it and was written by people with records. The unnamed comment cannot be traced. This matches the lesson skill for source.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","The school history, because it names who made it and was written by people with records. The unnamed comment cannot be traced.","Copying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Which source is more reliable for the date a school opened: a school history written by the school, or a comment on a website with no author?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Copying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Good sources are chosen by asking who made them, when, why and whether they can be trusted. Each source is recorded with its title, author or creator, date…","The school history, because it names who made it and was written by people with records. The unnamed comment cannot be traced.","Careful use of record and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Copying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising source?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named record and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the record and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising source. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The school history, because it names who made it and was written by people with records. The unnamed comment cannot be traced. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["The school history, because it names who made it and was written by people with records. The unnamed comment cannot be traced.","Copying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about source?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about source.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Copying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about record and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Example source record: Title: \"Main Street, 1985\" photograph; Creator: unknown, held by the local history room; Date: 1985; Where found:…","Copying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Good sources are chosen by asking who made them, when, why and whether they can be trusted. Each source is… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Copying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own.","Find three sources for your inquiry question. Complete a source record for each with creator, date and where found, and write one paraphrased fact…","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of source?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating source on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: The school history, because it names who made it and was written by people with records. The unnamed comment cannot be traced. Reasoning: I used source by attending to record and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with source, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Copying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own. Correction: The school history, because it names who made it and was written by people with records. The unnamed comment cannot be traced. Why: the correction restores accurate use of source.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w37-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise source.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"Historical and Geographical Inquiry","success_criteria":["I can use source with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify record and explain why it matters.","I can practise author independently on a new prompt.","I can notice paraphrase needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Good sources are chosen by asking who made them, when, why and whether they can be trusted. Each source is recorded with its title, author or creator, date and where it was found. Paraphrasing means putting ideas in your own words and naming the source, instead of copying.…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Good sources are chosen by asking who made them, when, why and whether they can be trusted. Each source is recorded with its title, author or creator, date and where it was found. Paraphrasing means putting ideas in your own words and naming the source, instead of copying.","A claim unrelated to the source or place."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about source?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample source record: Title: \"Main Street, 1985\" photograph; Creator: unknown, held by the local history room; Date: 1985; Where found: local library; What it shows: a bakery and a bus stop. Paraphrase: \"The photo shows a bakery next to a bus stop.\"\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhich source is more reliable for the date a school opened: a school history written by the school, or a comment on a website with no author?\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe school history, because it names who made it and was written by people with records. The unnamed comment cannot be traced.","explain":"Good sources are chosen by asking who made them, when, why and whether they can be trusted. Each source is recorded with its title, author or creator, date and where it was found. Paraphrasing means putting ideas in your own words and naming the source, instead of copying.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Copying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"A source record and paraphrase.","caption":"Record every source.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Record</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">title, creator, date, where</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Paraphrase</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">own words and name the source</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example source record: Title: \"Main Street, 1985\" photograph; Creator: unknown, held by the local history room; Date: 19 Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example source record: Title: \"Main Street, 1985\" photograph; Creator: unknown, held by the local history room; Date: 19 Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about source?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample source record: Title: \"Main Street, 1985\" photograph; Creator: unknown, held by the local history room; Date: 1985; Where found: local library; What it shows: a bakery and a bus stop. Paraphrase: \"The photo shows a bakery next to a bus stop.\" Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Reliable and unclear sources.","caption":"Ask who made it and when.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Reliable</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">named maker, records</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Unknown</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">comment with no author</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about source?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCopying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own. Here is a more productive model: Example source record: Title: \"Main Street, 1985\" photograph; Creator: unknown, held by the local history room; Date: 1985; Where found: local library; What it shows: a bakery and a bus stop. Paraphrase: \"The photo shows a bakery next to a bus stop.\"\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe school history, because it names who made it and was written by people with records. The unnamed comment cannot be traced.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Copying sentences without naming the source passes off someone else''s work as your own. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about source?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nGood sources are chosen by asking who made them, when, why and whether they can be trusted. Each source is recorded with its title, author or creator, date and where it was found. Paraphrasing means putting ideas in your own words and naming the source, instead of copying. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 37, Lesson 1: Finding and recording sources","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Inquiry project","week":37,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample source record: Title: \"Main Street, 1985\" photograph; Creator: unknown, held by the local history room; Date: 1985; Where found: local library; What it shows: a bakery and a bus stop. Paraphrase: \"The photo shows a bakery next to a bus stop.\"\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A source record and paraphrase.","caption":"Record every source.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Record</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">title, creator, date, where</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Paraphrase</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">own words and name the source</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Reliable and unclear sources.","caption":"Ask who made it and when.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Reliable</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">named maker, records</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Unknown</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">comment with no author</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-hsie-y3-w37-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
